# Cloudflare Pages reads this file, applies the rules and does not serve it.
#
# Everything here revalidates: the browser still gets a fast 304 when nothing
# changed, but an edit to restaurants.json shows up on the next load rather
# than whenever a cache decides to expire. Photos are the one exception.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

# The pages carry the ?v= stamps that tell a browser which copy of the scripts
# and stylesheets it is allowed to reuse, so a stale page is a stale
# everything. These rules match the request path, and the address people
# actually open is "/", not "/index.html", so both are spelled out.
#
# "/" is answered by functions/index.js on every visit — the map with its head
# written for the language and the place the address names, and the places
# written into it as text. Unlike the /split and /lists routes below, that one
# does not write headers of its own: it takes the asset server's answer, this
# rule and the two under /* on it, and hands it back with the head and the
# text swapped, so there is one place saying how the map caches and this is
# it. Minus the ETag, which described the file and not the answer: what a
# browser loses is the 304, and the header of that file says why. No noindex
# — the map is the page everything else on this site is trying to be found
# for.
/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The lists page. Same revalidating rule as the map for the same reason — the
# ?v= stamps in it are what tell a browser which script to reuse — plus a
# noindex, because there is nothing on it for anybody who is not signed in and
# a search result for "your lists" would be a permanently empty page.
#
# /list/<id> and /lists are not here: they are served by
# functions/list/[id].js and functions/lists/index.js, which set their own
# headers. Both are indexable — a public list is a page somebody wrote and
# /lists is what links them together — and neither is disallowed in
# robots.txt. See the note there, and the head of each Function.
#
# This rule is one path and not two, which every other page in this file
# spells both ways. Pages serves lists.html at /lists as well, the way it
# serves feedback.html at /feedback — but /lists is the directory now, answered by
# a Function that outranks the static file and sets its own headers, so the
# static spelling never binds there and a noindex written for it would be a
# line about a page nobody is served.
/lists.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

# The account page. Same revalidating rule and the same noindex as the lists
# page, and for stronger reasons: everything on it is one session's own, a
# search result for it would be the sign-in offer for everybody who was not
# the person who signed in, and there is nothing on it a crawler could reach
# in the first place. account.html carries the meta robots tag to match.
/account.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# The editor for the page under your name, one door off the account page and
# behind the same session, so the same two lines for the same reasons.
# Spelled both ways, because /edit is the address the account page links to.
/edit
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

/edit.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# How the page under your name is doing, one door off the account page and
# behind the same session, so the same two lines for the same reasons — and
# spelled both ways for the reason /edit is.
/insights
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

/insights.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# Splitwise. Stored as split.html and served at /split as well — and, on
# splitwise.tallinntastebuds.ee, at the bare root, which
# functions/_middleware.js rewrites to this file. These rules match the
# request path, so both spellings are spelled out, the same reason "/" and
# "/index.html" both appear at the top of this file.
#
# Neither rule is the one that usually binds any more. functions/split.js
# serves this page and sets its own Cache-Control and X-Robots-Tag, the way
# the three routes that serve lists.html do — see page() in
# functions/_shell.js. These stay for the case where that route is not
# reached, and they are what the subdomain's own root would otherwise fall
# through to: it matches "/" further up, which carries no noindex at all.
#
# Same revalidating Cache-Control as the map, for the ?v= stamps, and the same
# noindex the account page has, for the same reason: everything here is one
# session's own, and a search result for it would be the sign-in offer shown
# to everybody who is not the person who signed in.
/split.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

/split
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# Flashcards. Stored as flashcard.html and served at /flashcard as well — and,
# on flashcard.tallinntastebuds.ee, at the bare root, which
# functions/_middleware.js rewrites to this path. These rules match the request
# path, so both spellings are spelled out, the same reason "/" and
# "/index.html" both appear at the top of this file.
#
# Same revalidating Cache-Control as the map on both, for the ?v= stamps. What
# differs between the two spellings is the robots tag, and it is the same split
# /lists.html and /lists are under further up.
#
# /flashcard is answered by functions/flashcard.js, which sets its own
# Cache-Control and X-Robots-Tag through page() in functions/_shell.js — index
# for a deck the site ships, noindex for one somebody wrote, which is a thing
# only the route can tell apart. The rule here is what is left for the case
# where that route is not reached, and it carries no robots tag so that it
# cannot contradict the route's.
#
# /flashcard.html is the same page at the address nobody is given, and it gets
# the noindex, because two indexable addresses for one page is a search engine
# picking one and half the links pointing at the other. It is the only spelling
# that can be indexed by accident: the route never answers here, so nothing
# writes a canonical into the head, and what a crawler would file is the static
# fallback with the page's own tags on it.
#
# This page carried noindex on both spellings for a day. **Flashcards** in
# README.md says what changed and why unlinked is not the same as hidden.
/flashcard.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

/flashcard
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The owner's pages: the statistics and Google's directory of the city, stored
# as admin/stats.html and admin/google.html and served at /admin/stats and
# /admin/google as well. functions/_middleware.js answers 404 to anybody who
# is not signed in as an account ADMINS names in wrangler.toml, and sends the
# owner's copy `private, no-store`; this says the same for every spelling
# under the prefix, so the two never disagree about whether a cache may keep
# it. `noindex, nofollow`, the strongest on the site, because nothing here is
# for a search result: the directory is somebody else's description of
# Tallinn, and the statistics are how this site is used.
/admin/*
  Cache-Control: private, no-store
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# The blog. Stored as blog.html and served at /blog as well, so both
# spellings are spelled out, the same reason "/" and "/index.html" both appear
# at the top of this file. The address people are given is the short one.
#
# Same revalidating Cache-Control as the map, for the ?v= stamps — and no
# X-Robots-Tag at all, deliberately. Nothing on this site links to the blog,
# which would ordinarily put it in the same bracket as /google below; it is
# the opposite case. This is a post per thing the map does, in this site's own
# words, and being found by somebody searching for one of those questions is
# most of what it is for. Unlinked and indexed, rather than unlinked and
# hidden — robots.txt has the argument in full. The posts themselves are
# ?post=<id> on this same path, so they are covered by this rule and by the
# canonical assets/blog.js writes for each of them.
/blog.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/blog
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Feedback. Stored as feedback.html and served at /feedback as well, so both
# spellings are spelled out, the same reason "/" and "/index.html" both appear
# at the top of this file. The address people are given is the short one.
#
# Same revalidating Cache-Control as the map, for the ?v= stamps, and a
# noindex — but `follow` rather than the account page's `nofollow`. Everything
# on this page is written about this site rather than about the city, and a
# search for Tallinn Tastebuds answered with its own snag list would put the
# map's worst page in front of its best; the bylines on it, though, lead to
# real profiles, and there is no reason a crawler should not walk to them.
# The page carries the meta robots tag to match; this is the half that binds
# for anything that does not parse the HTML to find it.
/feedback.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

/feedback
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

# Scripts and stylesheets are content-addressed by tools/stamp.mjs — the URL
# in the HTML carries a hash of the file — so a year-long cache would be safe
# for a stamped request and is deliberately not used anyway. These rules match
# on path and ignore the query string, so the same header would also be handed
# to a request for the bare, unstamped assets/app.js, and a browser that had
# pinned that one for a year would be stuck there for a year. Revalidating
# costs one conditional request and cannot strand anybody.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The one asset whose URL cannot carry a ?v= stamp: browsers and Google's
# favicon crawler both ask for the bare path. Revalidating is how a redrawn
# mark ever reaches a tab that has already seen the old one.
/favicon.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The file you actually edit. This one must never go stale.
/data/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The blog's clips. A week would be wrong here and it is the one picture
# directory where it would: a clip is drawn from a scene by
# tools/blogclips.mjs and redrawn under the same name whenever that scene
# changes, so a cached copy is a copy of a scene nobody can see any more.
# Revalidating costs one conditional request and cannot strand anybody, which
# is the same argument /data/* is under.
/clips/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Photos are named per place and are replaced rather than edited, so they can
# sit in the cache for a week.
/photos/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# A story's video is written once, watched for a day or two and then deleted.
# Nothing ever edits one in place, so the file at a given name is always the
# same file, and a browser that has it should keep it: this is the largest
# thing on the site and the one most likely to be watched twice.
/stories/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# The discount pages turn over an hourly code between them, so a cached copy
# is a wrong copy. They are also the three pages that must never be indexed
# or shown in a preview card, whatever a link unfurler decides to do with the
# noindex tag in the markup.
/deal.html
  Cache-Control: no-store
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

/verify.html
  Cache-Control: no-store
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

/staff.html
  Cache-Control: no-store
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# The admin page holds a token in the browser it is opened in. A cached copy
# is a copy of the door, so there is never one.
/admin.html
  Cache-Control: no-store
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
